*
* START-HISTORY:
* 7 Nov 26 dm   S.168 (the owner: "sdsys should start at the prompt always"): the administrator returns before the start setting is read, so it always starts at
*               the prompt; S.167's exemption inside the sign-out block (the same administrator, a window too small) is folded into it.
* 7 Nov 26 dm   S.167 (the owner: "Exempt sdsys"): the administrator is left at the prompt when the home screen could not open, not signed out.
* 7 Nov 26 dm   S.164 (the owner: "where the default is to open an account in home, the user should not be able to get to the TCL prompt by just having a window
*               that is too small"; asked whether to sign out even when the Terminal tile is on: "Yes, always sign out"): when the home screen could not OPEN
*               (hrefused, set by tool_home: a window too small, or no screen), the session is signed out with message 15015 whatever the tiles say.
* 28 Oct 26 dm  S.87: CLOSESEQ and EQU are gone as statements: this program says CLOSE and EQUATE.
* 22 Oct 26 dm  New (S.75, H2; the owner: "terminal or menu determined by admin for each account (another gate) - default menu").
* END-HISTORY
*
* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*   call !home_entry(why)
*
*     why   signon (the main loop of the command processor is about to start) or logto (a LOGTO typed at the prompt has entered an account and run its LOGIN)
*
*   Starts the HOME verb when the account's setting says menu.  CPROC calls it from those two places and nowhere else, and only on a terminal.
*
*   The setting: field 16 of the account's register record (ACC$START) is menu or terminal; BLANK follows the system default, the one word in the file
*   start.default in the sdsys directory (SET.START.DEFAULT writes it; menu when the file is absent or says anything else).  An account with no register
*   record (one entered by a path) follows the default too.
*
*   It starts nothing when
*     - the session has no terminal (@tty does not begin /dev/: piped input, a phantom, telnet and the rest), so no command is ever read as a key;
*     - the process is an API server;
*     - the LOGTO is run by the home screen itself (hlogto of the named common /TKHOME/), which shows the new account's screen itself;
*     - why is logto and the command processor is not the bottom one (a LOGTO run by a program with EXECUTE);
*     - the account's VOC has no HOME entry (an account not brought up to date).
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine home_entry(why)
$catalog !home_entry

$include keys.h
$include int$keys.h

   common /tkhome/ hflag, hlogto, hstart, hrefused

   if @tty[1, 5] # '/dev/' then return
   if kernel(K$IS.SDAPISRVR, 0) then return
   if hlogto then return
   if why = 'logto' then
      if kernel(K$CPROC.LEVEL, 0) > 1 then return
   end
* S.168 (the owner, 7 Nov 26: "sdsys should start at the prompt always"): the administrator never starts at the home screen, at sign-on or after a LOGTO, whatever
*   the system's default; HOME typed at the prompt still opens it.  This also covers S.167's case (a window too small): there is no screen to refuse.
   if kernel(K$ADMINISTRATOR, -1) then return

   start = ''
   openpath @sdsys : @ds : 'accounts' to acc.f then
      read arec from acc.f, downcase(@who) then start = downcase(trim(arec<ACC$START>))
      close acc.f
   end
   if start # 'menu' and start # 'terminal' then
      start = 'menu'
      openseq @sdsys : @ds : 'start.default' readonly to df then      ;* READONLY: without it an absent file is created by the open
         readseq line from df then
            if downcase(trim(line)) = 'terminal' then start = 'terminal'
         end
         close df
      end else
*        26 Oct 26 (found on the VM, S76-b2): THE DELIVERED STATE HAS NO start.default.  OPENSEQ of an absent file takes ELSE and still holds a lock on the name until the file variable is closed
*        (which is how a program creates a file), so every session that started at the home screen kept an update lock on start.default for its whole life and the NEXT session waited at
*        sign-on until the first one ended.  Close it here.
         if not(unassigned(df)) then close df
      end
   end
   if start # 'menu' then return

   openpath 'voc' to vf else return
   read vrec from vf, 'home' else vrec = ''
   close vf
   if vrec = '' then return

   hrefused = 0
   hstart = 1                                  ;* 3 Nov 26: this screen is the session's own (the account starts in the menu), not one typed at the prompt: Ctrl-Q signs out
   execute 'home'

* S.164: a screen that could not OPEN (a window too small, no screen) is never the way to the prompt for an account that starts in the menu, whatever its tiles:
*   the person is signed out, and says so.  Only the opening counts: what ends a screen that did open is decided below, by the Terminal tile, as before.
   if hrefused then
      display sysmsg(15015)                    ;* This account starts at the home screen, which could not open, so the session is signed out
      sleep 2
      execute 'quit'
   end

* An account whose Terminal tile the administrator has turned off is not given the prompt that the screen was the way out of: whatever ended the screen (a window too
*   small, an error, the cursor lock), the person is signed out.  The prompt is the account's own choice only when it STARTS there (START-TERMINAL), and then we are not here.
   call !home_off(@who, off)
   if index(' ' : off : ' ', ' terminal ', 1) then
      sleep 2
      execute 'quit'
   end
   return
end

* END-CODE
